.TH "LANGUAGEMODEL.CREATE_STATIC" "3JS" "August 27, 2026" "JavaScript" "JavaScript Reference Manual"
.SH NAME
LanguageModel.create_static \- LanguageModel: create() static method
.SH SYNOPSIS
\f[B]Secure context:\f[R] This feature is available only in secure
contexts (HTTPS).
.PP
The \f[B]\f[CB]create()\f[B]\f[R] static method of the
\f[CR]LanguageModel\f[R] interface constructs a new
\f[CR]LanguageModel\f[R] instance, automatically downloading the
corresponding model data if it is not already available.
.SH SYNTAX
.IP
.EX
LanguageModel.create()
LanguageModel.create(options)
.EE
.SS Parameters
.TP
\f[B]options\f[R] \f[I](optional)\f[R]
An object representing the options for creating a
\f[CR]LanguageModel\f[R] session.
Properties include:
.RS
.TP
\f[B]expectedInputs\f[R]
An array of objects representing the required input modalities and
languages.
Each object can include the following properties:
.RS
.TP
\f[B]type\f[R]
An enumerated value indicating the content type.
Must be one of:
.RS
.TP
\f[B]text\f[R]
Plain text content.
.TP
\f[B]image\f[R]
Image content.
.TP
\f[B]audio\f[R]
Audio content.
.TP
\f[B]tool\-call\f[R]
A tool invocation issued by the model.
.TP
\f[B]tool\-response\f[R]
The result of a tool invocation.
.RE
.TP
\f[B]languages\f[R] \f[I](optional)\f[R]
An array of strings containing \c
.UR https://www.rfc-editor.org/info/rfc5646/
BCP 47
.UE \c
\ language tags (for example, \f[CR]en\f[R], \f[CR]fr\f[R],
\f[CR]ja\f[R]) that the session is expected to handle for this content
type.
The user agent uses this list to determine whether the model supports
the specified languages and to select appropriate model components or
fine\-tunings.
.RE
.TP
\f[B]expectedOutputs\f[R]
An array of objects representing the required output modalities and
languages.
Each object can include the following properties:
.RS
.TP
\f[B]type\f[R]
An enumerated value indicating the content type.
Must be one of:
.RS
.TP
\f[B]text\f[R]
Plain text content.
.TP
\f[B]image\f[R]
Image content.
.TP
\f[B]audio\f[R]
Audio content.
.TP
\f[B]tool\-call\f[R]
A tool invocation issued by the model.
.TP
\f[B]tool\-response\f[R]
The result of a tool invocation.
.RE
.TP
\f[B]languages\f[R] \f[I](optional)\f[R]
An array of strings containing \c
.UR https://www.rfc-editor.org/info/rfc5646/
BCP 47
.UE \c
\ language tags (for example, \f[CR]en\f[R], \f[CR]fr\f[R],
\f[CR]ja\f[R]) that the session is expected to handle for this content
type.
The user agent uses this list to determine whether the model supports
the specified languages and to select appropriate model components or
fine\-tunings.
.RE
.TP
\f[B]initialPrompts\f[R]
An array of objects representing messages passed during the creation of
a language model session.
This allows the model to \(lqremember\(rq instructions or previous
dialogue without resending them with every new query.
Each object can include the following properties:
.RS
.TP
\f[B]role\f[R]
A string indicating the point of view the message is phrased from.
Must be one of:
.RS
.TP
\f[B]system\f[R]
A system\-level instruction that guides the model\(cqs overall behavior.
This must be the first instruction passed to the model.
.TP
\f[B]user\f[R]
A message from the user, which the API should respond to.
.TP
\f[B]assistant\f[R]
An input that provides context for the AI assistant, such as its persona
or the format of its responses.
Such messages mainly serve to provide context/history, and further shape
how the model responds.
.RE
.TP
\f[B]content\f[R]
A string representing a textual prompt, or an array of objects.
Each object includes the following properties:
.RS
.TP
\f[B]type\f[R]
An enumerated value representing the type of content.
This can be one of:
.RS
.TP
\f[B]audio\f[R]
Audio content.
.TP
\f[B]image\f[R]
Image content.
.TP
\f[B]text\f[R]
Textual content.
.TP
\f[B]tool\-call\f[R]
A tool invocation issued by the model.
.TP
\f[B]tool\-response\f[R]
The result of a tool invocation.
.RE
.TP
\f[B]value\f[R]
The content of the message.
If the \f[CR]type\f[R] is \f[CR]text\f[R], this is always a string.
If the \f[CR]type\f[R] is \f[CR]audio\f[R] or \f[CR]image\f[R], the
\f[CR]value\f[R] can be one of several different object types; see What
data types are accepted?.
.RE
.TP
\f[B]prefix\f[R] \f[I](optional)\f[R]
A boolean, defaulting to \f[CR]false\f[R].
When \f[CR]true\f[R], the message is treated as a prefix for the
model\(cqs next generated response rather than a complete turn.
.RE
.TP
\f[B]monitor\f[R]
A reference to a \f[CR]CreateMonitor\f[R] callback function to receive
download progress events.
.TP
\f[B]signal\f[R]
An \f[CR]AbortSignal\f[R] to cancel session creation.
.TP
\f[B]tools\f[R]
An array of objects representing tools available to the AI.
Each object can include the following properties:
.RS
.TP
\f[B]name\f[R]
A string giving the tool a unique name the model uses to refer to it
when issuing a tool call.
.TP
\f[B]description\f[R]
A string describing what the tool does.
The model uses this description to decide if and when to invoke the
tool.
.TP
\f[B]inputSchema\f[R]
A \c
.UR https://json-schema.org/
JSON Schema
.UE \c
\ that describes the tool\(cqs input parameters.
The model uses this schema to construct the arguments it passes to the
tool\(cqs \f[CR]execute\f[R] function.
.TP
\f[B]execute\f[R]
A callback function that the user agent invokes when the model calls
this tool.
Its arguments are specific to the model being used.
It must return a \f[CR]Promise\f[R] that resolves with a
\f[CR]String\f[R] representing the tool\(cqs result.
.RE
.RE
.SS Return value
A \f[CR]Promise\f[R] that resolves with a new \f[CR]LanguageModel\f[R]
instance.
.SS Exceptions
.TP
\f[B]AbortError\f[R] \f[B]DOMException\f[R]
Thrown if the operation was aborted via the \f[CR]signal\f[R] option.
.TP
\f[B]InvalidStateError\f[R] \f[B]DOMException\f[R]
Thrown if the calling document is not fully active.
.TP
\f[B]NotAllowedError\f[R] \f[B]DOMException\f[R]
Thrown if usage of the method is blocked by a \f[CR]language\-model\f[R]
\f[CR]Permissions\-Policy\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]NotSupportedError\f[R] \f[B]DOMException\f[R]
Thrown if:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
A message\(cqs \f[CR]role\f[R] is \f[CR]assistant\f[R] and its
\f[CR]type\f[R] is anything other than \f[CR]text\f[R].
.IP \(bu 2
A message\(cqs \f[CR]type\f[R] is \f[CR]text\f[R] and its
\f[CR]value\f[R] is not a string.
.IP \(bu 2
The input or output text is in a language the user agent doesn\(cqt
support for prompting.
.IP \(bu 2
A message\(cqs \f[CR]type\f[R] is \f[CR]image\f[R] or \f[CR]audio\f[R]
but the type was not listed in \f[CR]expectedInputs\f[R], or the
\f[CR]value\f[R] is not an accepted data type.
.RE
.TP
\f[B]OperationError\f[R] \f[B]DOMException\f[R]
Thrown if creation fails for any other reason not listed in the other
exception types.
.TP
\f[B]QuotaExceededError\f[R] \f[B]DOMException\f[R]
Thrown if the content provided in \f[CR]initialPrompts\f[R] exceeds the
model\(cqs \f[CR]LanguageModel.contextWindow\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]SyntaxError\f[R] \f[B]DOMException\f[R]
Thrown if:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
No messages are included in the messages array.
.IP \(bu 2
A message\(cqs \f[CR]prefix\f[R] property is set to \f[CR]true\f[R] and:
.RS 2
.IP \(bu 2
The message\(cqs \f[CR]role\f[R] is not \f[CR]assistant\f[R].
.IP \(bu 2
The message is not the last item in the messages array.
.RE
.RE
.TP
\f[B]TypeError\f[R] \f[B]DOMException\f[R]
Thrown if:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
A message\(cqs \f[CR]role\f[R] is \f[CR]system\f[R] but it was not the
first message passed to the context.
.RE
.SH DESCRIPTION
The \f[CR]create()\f[R] method constructs a new language model session,
automatically downloading the model if it is not already available.
You can monitor progress of a model download using the
\f[CR]monitor\f[R] option.
.PP
Before calling \f[CR]create()\f[R], use
\f[CR]LanguageModel.availability()\f[R] to check whether the desired
configuration is supported.
.PP
Once a session is created, use its instance methods \(em
\f[CR]LanguageModel.prompt()\f[R],
\f[CR]LanguageModel.promptStreaming()\f[R],
\f[CR]LanguageModel.append()\f[R], and others \(em to interact with the
model.
.SH SECURITY
Transient user activation is required.
The user has to interact with the page or a UI element for this feature
to work.
.SH EXAMPLES
.SS Creating a basic session
This example creates a default session and then prompts it for the
result of summing \f[CR]2\f[R] and \f[CR]2\f[R].
Note that text is supported by default, so the downloaded model should
be suitable for this case.
.IP
.EX
\f[B]const\f[R] session = \f[B]await\f[R] LanguageModel.create();
\f[B]const\f[R] answer = \f[B]await\f[R] session.prompt(\(dqWhat is 2 + 2?\(dq);
console.log(answer);
.EE
.PP
See also Using the Prompt API > Creating a \f[CR]LanguageModel\f[R]
session.
.SS Creating a session with a system prompt
The following example provides the AI with instructions on the persona
to adopt before generating an answer.
.IP
.EX
\f[B]const\f[R] session = \f[B]await\f[R] LanguageModel.create({
  initialPrompts: [
    {
      role: \(dqsystem\(dq,
      content: \(dqYou are a concise assistant. Respond in one sentence.\(dq,
    },
  ],
});

\f[B]const\f[R] response = \f[B]await\f[R] session.prompt(\(dqWhat is photosynthesis?\(dq);
console.log(response);
.EE
.PP
See also Adding context with initial and ongoing prompt inputs >
Providing initial prompts during session creation.
.SS Monitoring download progress
This code shows how you can monitor the download progress of a model.
Note that if the model is unavailable or already available, the event
will never fire.
.IP
.EX
\f[B]const\f[R] session = \f[B]await\f[R] LanguageModel.create({
  monitor(monitor) {
    monitor.addEventListener(\(dqdownloadprogress\(dq, ({ loaded, total }) \f[B]=>\f[R] {
      console.log(\(gaModel download: ${Math.round((loaded / total) * 100)}%\(ga);
    });
  },
});
.EE
.PP
See also Using the Prompt API > Monitoring download progress.
.SS Providing few\-shot prompts
The following example shows how to use a few\-shot prompt to ask the API
for a specific task (French translation) to be delivered in a specific
format, before providing some examples to help it learn the correct
output format.
.IP
.EX
\f[B]const\f[R] session = \f[B]await\f[R] LanguageModel.create({
  expectedInputs: [{ type: \(dqtext\(dq, languages: [\(dqen\(dq] }],
  expectedOutputs: [{ type: \(dqtext\(dq, languages: [\(dqen\(dq, \(dqfr\(dq] }],
  initialPrompts: [
    {
      role: \(dqsystem\(dq,
      content:
        \(dqTranslate the user\(aqs input to French. Use the output format \(aqEnglish input: French output\(aq\(dq,
    },
    { role: \(dquser\(dq, content: \(dqHello\(dq },
    { role: \(dqassistant\(dq, content: \(dqHello: Bonjour\(dq },
    { role: \(dquser\(dq, content: \(dqGoodbye\(dq },
    { role: \(dqassistant\(dq, content: \(dqGoodbye: Au revoir\(dq },
    { role: \(dquser\(dq, content: \(dqThe train is late\(dq },
    {
      role: \(dqassistant\(dq,
      content: \(dqThe train is late: Le train est en retard\(dq,
    },
    { role: \(dquser\(dq, content: \(dqMy shoes are pink\(dq },
    {
      role: \(dqassistant\(dq,
      content: \(dqMy shoes are pink: Mes chaussures sont roses\(dq,
    },
  ],
});

\f[B]const\f[R] result = \f[B]await\f[R] session.prompt(\(dqWindow\(dq);
console.log(result); \f[I]// \(dqWindow: Fenêtre\(dq\f[R]
.EE
.PP
See also Adding context with initial and ongoing prompt inputs >
Few\-shot prompts.
.SS Defining a tool with a callback
This example creates a session with a hypothetical \(lqget weather\(rq
tool.
When the model decides to call the tool, the user agent invokes
\f[CR]execute()\f[R] with the arguments the model provides.
.IP
.EX
\f[B]async\f[R] \f[B]function\f[R] getWeatherData(location) {
  \f[B]const\f[R] response = \f[B]await\f[R] fetch(
    \(gahttps://api.example.com/weather?city=${location}\(ga,
  );
  \f[B]const\f[R] data = \f[B]await\f[R] response.json();
  \f[B]return\f[R] \(ga${data.temp}°C, ${data.description}\(ga;
}

\f[B]const\f[R] session = \f[B]await\f[R] LanguageModel.create({
  tools: [
    {
      name: \(dqgetWeather\(dq,
      description: \(dqReturns the current weather for a given city.\(dq,
      inputSchema: {
        type: \(dqobject\(dq,
        properties: {
          location: { type: \(dqstring\(dq, description: \(dqThe city name.\(dq },
        },
        required: [\(dqlocation\(dq],
      },
      \f[B]async\f[R] execute(...args) {
        \f[B]const\f[R] location = args[0];
        \f[B]return\f[R] \f[B]await\f[R] getWeatherData(location);
      },
    },
  ],
});

\f[B]const\f[R] response = \f[B]await\f[R] session.prompt(\(dqWhat\(aqs the weather like in Tokyo?\(dq);
console.log(response);
.EE
.SS Canceling a session
The following example enables a user to cancel a prompt.
It does this by first creating an \f[CR]AbortController\f[R] and
assigning its \f[CR]abort()\f[R] method to a cancel button\(cqs click
handler.
Next, it calls \f[CR]create()\f[R] and passes
\f[CR]AbortController.signal\f[R] as the \f[CR]signal\f[R] property.
.IP
.EX
\f[B]const\f[R] controller = \f[B]new\f[R] AbortController();

\f[B]const\f[R] cancelButton = document.getElementById(\(dqcancel\-button\(dq);
cancelButton.addEventListener(\(dqclick\(dq, () \f[B]=>\f[R] controller.abort());

\f[B]const\f[R] session = \f[B]await\f[R] LanguageModel.create({
  signal: controller.signal,
  initialPrompts: [
    {
      role: \(dqsystem\(dq,
      content: \(dqYou are a helpful assistant.\(dq,
    },
  ],
});
.EE
.PP
See also Using the Prompt API > Canceling operations and destroying
instances.
